<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huancayo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Huancayo is a city in the central highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, capital of the Junín region, in the Mantaro valley at about 3,250 m. A Spanish town founded in the 16th century, it was where a congress adopted the Huancayo Constitution of 1839, and where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-castilla">Ramón Castilla</a> decreed the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in Peru in 1854. It is now the main commercial centre of central Peru.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huancayo.jpg" alt="Huancayo" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Candy Sotomayor · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plaza_de_la_Constituci%C3%B3n_Huancayo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
